  </div>
</section>

<!-- APPS -->
<section class="pane" id="apps" style="--c:var(--c-blue)">
  <div class="pane-head">
    <div class="slot rv" style="--k:0">U37-39</div>
    <h2 class="rv" style="--k:1">Apps people actually use</h2>
    <p class="lede rv" style="--k:2">Built for friends first, then opened up. Each one runs on the rack.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="entries">
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:3">
      <h3>Nostragoalus</h3>
      <p>A score-prediction game. Friends predict match scores, earn points for how close they get, and fight over per-competition and global leaderboards. Live crowd totals, bookmaker odds frozen at kickoff, one x2 joker per round, league chat. Football by default, and rugby union works too.</p>
      <div class="meta">TypeScript, Node or Bun</div>
      <div class="links"><a href="https://goal.arzaroth.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play</a><a href="https://github.com/Arzaroth/Nostragoalus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:4">
      <h3>La Coincherie</h3>
      <p>Play coinche with your friends when you live 800 km apart. Cameras on, your house rules.</p>
      <div class="meta">Card game, video</div>
      <div class="links"><a href="https://coinche.arzaroth.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sit at the table</a></div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:5">
      <h3>Yaek</h3>
      <p>Pulls Thai text apart so you can read what someone wrote to you. Offline dictionary, tones derived from the spelling, and no network request you did not ask for.</p>
      <div class="meta">Offline reading tool</div>
      <div class="links"><a href="https://yaek.arzaroth.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Yaek</a></div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:6">
      <h3>c2c-conv</h3>
      <p>Share one Claude Code session with a second person. They watch it live, and when you let them, they type into it as if they were you. The Cs in c2c could be Claude, Codex... or even clown. Ever heard of clown to clown conversation?</p>
      <div class="meta">TypeScript</div>
      <div class="links"><a href="https://github.com/Arzaroth/c2c-conv" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- GAUGES -->
<section class="pane" id="gauges" style="--c:var(--c-yellow)">
  <div class="pane-head">
    <div class="slot rv" style="--k:0">U34-35</div>
    <h2 class="rv" style="--k:1">Two gauges, one edge</h2>
    <p class="lede rv" style="--k:2">Rust binaries that draw panels on KDE Plasma, GNOME, Waybar and Quickshell. The two projects are unrelated, but they install and update themselves the same way. That shared code is selvedge.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pair">
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:3">
      <h3>TokenGauge</h3>
      <p>Shows usage limits, costs and token counts for Claude, Codex, Kimi, Grok, GLM and a few more. It predicts where each limit will be at reset and totals spend across all your machines.</p>
      <div class="meta">Waybar, Plasma, GNOME, Quickshell, TUI</div>
      <div class="links"><a href="https://github.com/Arzaroth/TokenGauge" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:4">
      <h3>TailGauge</h3>
      <p>Puts Tailscale and NetBird in your panel, and switches between them when both are installed. Turn the VPN on and off, and copy any machine's address. Tailscale also gets exit nodes, Mullvad regions and Taildrop. On NetBird you can join and leave networks. There's no daemon, it calls each provider's CLI.</p>
      <div class="meta">Plasma, GNOME, Quickshell, TUI</div>
      <div class="links"><a href="https://github.com/Arzaroth/TailGauge" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></div>
    </article>
  </div>
  <div class="selvedge rv" style="--k:5">
    <div class="selvedge-in">
      <div>
        <h3>selvedge</h3>
        <p>A selvedge is the woven edge that stops cloth from unravelling. This crate stops a binary and the QML and JavaScript it feeds from drifting apart. Both ship in one archive and update together.</p>
        <div class="links" style="--c:var(--c-yellow); margin-top:14px"><a href="https://github.com/Arzaroth/selvedge" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <ul>
          <li>Fetches the latest release from GitHub</li>
          <li>Replaces the running binary in place</li>
          <li>Reinstalls every desktop frontend from the same archive</li>
          <li>Keeps old command names alive as symlinks</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="lines-saved">Was <s>1,400 lines</s> in each gauge.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <article class="entry aside-gauge rv" style="--k:6">
    <h3>CodeGauge</h3>
    <p>Same family name, different job. It walks a repository's git history, counts every line once per day, and charts production code against test code over time. The dashboard is built into one static binary, and git doesn't need to be installed.</p>
    <div class="meta">Rust, gitoxide</div>
    <div class="links"><a href="https://git.arzaroth.com/Arzaroth/CodeGauge" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source on Forgejo</a></div>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- WORKBENCH -->
<section class="pane" id="workshop" style="--c:var(--c-green)">
  <div class="pane-head">
    <div class="slot rv" style="--k:0">U31-32</div>
    <h2 class="rv" style="--k:1">On the workbench</h2>
    <p class="lede rv" style="--k:2">Not public yet. They will be when they are ready.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="entries">
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:3">
      <h3>Tacite</h3>
      <p>Tracks contracts, subscriptions and warranties. It works out the legal deadlines, warns you while you can still act, and drafts the cancellation or warranty letter.</p>
      <div class="meta">TypeScript, Fastify, Postgres</div>
      <div class="links"><span class="wip">In progress</span></div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:4">
      <h3>Pouce Vert</h3>
      <p>A plant care app for the phone. Care sheets for common houseplants, pet toxicity with sources, watering reminders, and a diagnosis from a photo of the sad leaf.</p>
      <div class="meta">Expo, React Native</div>
      <div class="links"><span class="wip">In progress</span></div>
    </article>
    <article class="entry rv" style="--k:5">
      <h3>Sheaf</h3>
      <p>An open-source Android client for paperless-ngx, built against the v3 API so a server update does not break it. Document scanner with auto-crop, upload from any app's share sheet, offline-first with a draft queue, bulk inbox triage, and a real UI for server-side mail rules. Works behind reverse proxies, with self-signed certificates, mTLS, OIDC and several servers.</p>
      <div class="meta">Android first, iOS in development</div>
      <div class="links"><span class="wip">In progress</span></div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- OUROBOROS -->
<section class="pane" id="ouroboros" style="--c:var(--c-red)">
  <div class="pane-head">
    <div class="slot rv" style="--k:0">U26-29</div>
    <h2 class="rv" style="--k:1">ouroboros</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="ouro rv" style="--k:2">
    <svg class="snake" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="32" cy="32" r="26"></circle></svg>
    <p class="lede">What happens when a script that draws one small figure never gets told to stop. The first commit draws a lattice glyph in eight lines of Python. The latest one draws the same glyph.</p>
    <div class="scale" aria-label="Lines of code, drawn to scale">
      <div class="scale-row"><div><b>8</b><span>first commit</span></div><div class="bar tiny"></div></div>
      <div class="scale-row"><div><b data-count="25192">25,192</b><span>today, one file</span></div><div class="bar full"></div></div>
    </div>
    <ol class="tiers">
      <li class="rv" style="--k:4"><b>Reference</b>One digest per order. Every other tier is tested against it.</li>
      <li class="rv" style="--k:5"><b>A language for the glyph</b>A program computes the figure, through nineteen layers that include monads, a pass manager and an event-sourced rasteriser.</li>
      <li class="rv" style="--k:6"><b>LLVM</b>Lowers the bytecode to LLVM IR, JIT-compiles it and links a native ELF.</li>
      <li class="rv" style="--k:7"><b>A second language</b>General enough to write a compiler in. Its compiler is written in itself, and a three-generation fixpoint proves the build.</li>
      <li class="rv" style="--k:8"><b>The closed loop</b>The first front end, rewritten in the second language. Both emit the same bytes for any program.</li>
      <li class="rv" style="--k:9"><b>Machine code</b>Hand-encoded x86-64, aarch64 and riscv64 in a static ELF of about a kilobyte. No assembler, linker or libc.</li>
      <li class="rv" style="--k:10"><b>WebAssembly</b>The same module as a wasm reactor that imports nothing.</li>
      <li class="rv" style="--k:11"><b>No kernel</b>One boot sector walks the machine from 16-bit real mode to 64-bit and runs the glyph with nothing underneath.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="ouro-foot">
      <p>A differential fuzzer checks that every tier agrees.</p>
      <a href="https://github.com/Arzaroth/ouroboros" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read all 25,192 lines</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SELF-HOSTING -->
